      "claim": "Raltitrexed, a structurally related antifolate with a terminal glutamate, showed only 0.6–2% CSF penetration in nonhuman primates, demonstrating that this polar/ionized antifolate class has poor CSF access when the BBB is intact.",
      "evidence_role": "contradictory"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C10"],
      "claim": "A radiolabeled 5-methyltetrahydrofolate equilibrated into dog CSF within 3 h after IV bolus, showing that folate-type pteroyl structures can cross the blood-CSF barrier via carrier-mediated transport despite polar/ionized character.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C13"],
      "claim": "5-Methyltetrahydrofolate was recovered in dog CSF after IV administration against a concentration gradient, indicating a specific concentrative transport mechanism that can provide CNS access for folate analogues.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C12"],
      "claim": "[13C5]-5-formylTHF was detected in mouse brain after IV administration, confirming that folate analogues can be transported across the BBB in vivo.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "moderate",
  "decision_summary": "Although the query (methotrexate) is highly polar/ionized and would penetrate an intact BBB poorly, the disrupted-barrier condition and demonstrated carrier-mediated transport of folate analogues support meaningful CNS access after systemic administration; the contradictory antifolate CSF data reflect intact-barrier conditions and are outweighed.",
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct brain or CSF measurement of the query itself under disrupted-barrier conditions.",
    "The extent to which efflux transporters (BCRP/MRP) limit net brain uptake when the barrier is disrupted is not quantified.",
    "Transfer from reduced folates to the 2,4-diaminopteridine antifolate is indirect."
  ],
